```
ROLE: evaluation_prosecutor  VOTE: yellow
MANDATORY_BASELINES: [{"name": "Semantic RAG Caching (e.g., GPTCache)", "why": "Standard practice for redundant retrievals is to cache past (query, retrieved_span) pairs. If checking new queries against a query cache yields the same savings, checking against the 'live context window' specifically adds no structural novelty.", "repo_or_cite": "GPTCache / RAGCache"}]
STRAWMAN_RISKS: ["Comparing ONLY to random-skip is a mathematical sanity check, but operationally a strawman when standard query caching is the industry baseline for cutting RAG costs.", "Constructed trace with binary 'success iff needed item available' task accuracy ignores that LLMs can hallucinate correct answers even if skipped wrongly, or fail to extract the answer even if the span is present in the context."]
MISSING_METRICS: ["Cache-hit overlap (what fraction of the skipped retrievals would have simply been caught by a standard query cache)", "Check computation latency vs Window size W (Jaccard or embeddings over a large context window might eventually cost more time/compute than an optimized external cache lookup)"]
KILLER_EXPERIMENT: A head-to-head comparison on real agent RAG traces between the proposed 'in-context overlap gate' and a standard 'Semantic Query Cache' (using the same embedding model). If the semantic cache captures the same redundant retrievals at equal or better Pareto efficiency, the specific 'live window' novelty is falsified.
RATIONALE: The L0 honestly caveats the constructed nature of the traces and the matched-random control is a good basic guard, but it entirely misses that functionally, this mechanism competes with standard semantic caching. Beating random-skip is insufficient to prove the mechanism is better than a simple cache. The L1 MUST include a Semantic Cache baseline.
